name: permissions and identity
boss: Gatekeeper
flavor: Every door has a lock. I hold the modes.
brief: Every file has three sets of permissions -- owner, group, everyone --
brief: and three bits in each: read, write, execute. ls -l shows them as
brief: rwxr-xr-x. chmod changes them.
brief:
brief: Numeric modes are the fast way once they click: r=4, w=2, x=1, added up
brief: per set. 755 is rwxr-xr-x, 644 is rw-r--r--, 600 is rw------- .
brief:
brief: On xos everything runs as root and the root filesystem is read-only, so
brief: these matter for what you write to tmpfs and to disks you mount -- not
brief: for defending this machine from you.
---
q: %make% @script@ executable
a: chmod +x @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: set @script@ to mode 755 -- everyone may read and run, only you may write
a: chmod 755 @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: set @txt@ to mode 644
a: chmod 644 @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: set @txt@ so only you can read or write it, nobody else anything
a: chmod 600 @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: remove the execute bit from @script@ for everyone
a: chmod -x @script@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: add write permission for the group on @txt@
a: chmod g+w @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: take away all permissions from everyone-else on @txt@
a: chmod o= @txt@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod
---
q: %make% every file under @dir@ readable by everyone, recursively
a: chmod -R a+r @dir@
ref: chmod
teach: chmod -R
---
q: who are you
a: whoami
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: whoami
teach: whoami
---
q: %show% your user id, group id and groups all at once
a: id
out: uid=
ref: id
teach: id
---
q: %show% just your numeric user id
a: id -u
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -u
---
q: %show% just your user NAME rather than the number
a: id -un
out: ^[a-z_][a-z0-9_-]*$
ref: id
teach: id -n
---
q: %show% just your numeric group id
a: id -g
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -g
---
q: %show% every group you belong to, numerically
a: id -G
out: [0-9]
ref: id
teach: id -G
---
q: %show% your real user id rather than the effective one
a: id -ur
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: id
teach: id -r
---
q: %show% the default permission mask new files are created with
a: umask
out: [0-7]
ref: umask
teach: umask
---
q: set the mask so new files are private to you
a: umask 077
ref: umask
teach: umask
---
q: %show% everything the filesystem knows about @txt@
a: stat @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat
---
q: %show% just the size of @txt@ in bytes, nothing else
a: stat -c %s @txt@
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
hint: -c takes a format; %s is size, %n name, %a permission bits
---
q: %show% the permission bits of @script@ as a number
a: stat -c %a @script@
out: ^[0-9]+$
ref: stat
teach: stat -c
---
q: %show% filesystem information for @txt@ rather than the file itself
a: stat -f @txt@
out: ID|Block
ref: stat
teach: stat -f
---
q: stat the target of a symlink rather than the link
a: stat -L @txt@
out: Size
ref: stat
teach: stat -L
---
q: %show% stat's output in terse single-line form
a: stat -t @txt@
out: [0-9]
ref: stat
teach: stat -t
---
q: set @txt@'s timestamp to a date you name rather than to now
a: touch -d 2024-03-01 @txt@
ref: touch
teach: touch -d
---
q: give @txt2@ the same timestamp @txt@ already has
a: touch -r @txt@ @txt2@
ref: touch
teach: touch -r
